L78: 【48†View all resources】\nL79: \nL80:   * Open Source\nL81: \nL82:     * COMMUNITY\nL83:       * 【49†GitHub SponsorsFund open source developers】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTAlloc: Enhancing Memory Efficiency in Large-Scale Model Training with Spatio-Temporal Planning (https://arxiv.org/html/2507.16274v2)\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"pattern\":\"lifetime\"}); Total lines: 458\nNo matching text found for \"lifetime\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTAlloc: Enhancing Memory Efficiency in Large-Scale Model Training with Spatio-Temporal Planning (https://arxiv.org/html/2507.16274v2)\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"pattern\":\"synchronization\"}); Total lines: 458\nL372: As a result, dynamic requests can reuse idle regions in the static pool, reducing overall peak usage.\nL373: ## 10. Related Work\nL374: Online GPU Allocators. To reduce memory fragmentation and improve allocation efficiency, a plethora of online GPU memory allocators (【93†Adinetz and Pleiter, 2014】; 【94†Widmer et al., 2013】; 【95†Steinberger et al., 2012】; 【96†Vinkler et al., 2014】) have been developed. Dynamic allocators operate atop the native GPU memory APIs (e.g., cudaMalloc) in a similar manner as the caching allocator of PyTorch.\nL375: Differently, such allocators are meant to run on GPU threads alongside GPGPU applications, rather than managing GPU memory from the host like PyTorch and GMLake. To reduce fragmentation, they usually adopt sophisticated allocation policies such as the slab and buddy systems. Also, to achieve high-throughput allocations on GPUs, they have proposed scalable synchronization primitives across the massive threads of GPUs (【97†Gelado and Garland, 2019】).\nL376: As a pluggable allocator of PyTorch, STAlloc also chooses to manage GPU memory from the host to improve usability and programmability.\nL377: Generic Memory Defragmentation Techniques. Memory defragmentation has been studied and discussed in various scenarios (【98†Aigner et al., 2015】; 【99†Johnstone and Wilson, 1998】; 【100†Krauss et al., 2023】) beyond GPU applications. Previous work (【101†Veldema and Philippsen, 2012】; 【102†Marlow et al., 2008】; 【103†Hudson and Moss, 2001】; 【104†Siegwart and Hirzel, 2006】) has proposed defragmentation strategy based on data movement or copying.\nL378: These approaches are mainly deployed in the real-time system with unpredictable runtime behaviors, which result in complex defragmentation strategies with high runtime overhead.\nL379: Machine Learning Compilers. Machine Learning compilers that convert high-level computation graphs to GPU instructions must manage memory allocation for these graphs. The compilers pre-analyze control dependencies to optimize memory layouts before execution. Compilers like TVM (【105†Chen et al., 2018】) and TFLite (【106†David et al., 2021】) use greedy heuristics for reasonable allocation, while Checkmate (【107†Jain et al., 2020】) employs solvers for optimal rematerialization to improve results.\nL380: Unlike deep ML compilers that organize memory allocation and deallocation at the computation graph level, STAlloc manages memory requests at the level of the overall model execution. These approaches are complementary and orthogonal with our work.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2507.16274] STAlloc: Enhancing Memory Efficiency in Large-Scale Model Training with Spatio-Temporal Planning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16274)\nciteturn6view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"Submission history\"}); Total lines: 173\nL18: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and C